course: chemistry
lesson: 30
title: Glass and pottery
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A window pane is made of sand. Sand is melted with soda and crushed limestone in a furnace hotter than 1000 °C, and the glowing runny glass is cooled into sheets, bottles and jars.
explain: When glass cools, its particles do not have time to line up in neat crystals; they freeze in a jumble, so glass is a hard solid that breaks with smooth, razor-sharp edges.
explain: Hot glass is soft like toffee: a glassblower blows into a blob of it on a long pipe and it swells into a bubble that becomes a vase. Nature makes glass too, when lightning strikes sand or lava cools very fast.
explain: Pottery is different: soft clay is shaped and then baked in a very hot oven called a kiln until it turns hard as stone. A shiny glaze, which is a thin coat of glass, makes a mug waterproof.
fact: Ordinary glass is made by melting sand with soda and limestone; people first made glass thousands of years ago.
fact: In glass the particles are frozen in a jumble instead of neat crystals; old windows that are thicker at the bottom were made that way, glass does not slowly flow down.
fact: Glassblowers shape glass that is over 1000 °C, when it is soft and stretchy.
fact: Oven glass expands very little when heated, so it does not crack in the oven; ordinary glass can crack when heated or cooled unevenly, for example a hot dish put into cold water.
fact: Lightning striking sand can melt it into glass tubes called fulgurites; volcanoes make a natural black glass called obsidian.
fact: Sea glass is broken glass rolled by waves and sand for many years, often decades, until its edges are smooth and frosty.
fact: Broken glass splits into thin, sharp edges, so it is swept up carefully with a brush and dustpan, never picked up with bare hands.
fact: Car side and back windows are toughened to crumble into small blunt pieces; the windscreen has a layer of plastic inside, so it cracks but holds together.
fact: Pottery is clay baked in a kiln at a very high heat, which makes it hard for good; a glaze is a thin glass coating that makes it waterproof; a clay pot must dry out fully before firing, or the water inside turns to steam and can crack it.
fact: Glass can be melted and made into new bottles again and again without wearing out, and melting old glass needs less energy than making it from sand.
fact: Phone screens are toughened glass, treated so the surface is harder; a drop on a corner can still crack them.
fact: Optical fibres are thin strands of glass that carry light along bends, because the light keeps bouncing inside the fibre; most of the internet travels through such fibres, even under the oceans.
exercise: What is ordinary window glass mostly made from? | Sand melted with soda and limestone | Water frozen very, very hard in a factory | Melted plastic bottles | Crushed shells and salt
exercise: Why does broken glass have such sharp edges? | It splits cleanly into thin edges | It has tiny hooks along each edge | It is coated with metal dust | It is still slightly melted inside
exercise: How does a glassblower make a vase? | Blows into a blob of hot, soft glass | Pours cold glass into a sock | Carves it out of a block of ice | Sticks glass beads together with glue
exercise: Why does an oven-glass jug not crack in the oven? | It expands very little when heated | It is much thicker than a house brick | It is coated in a thin layer of oil | It is not really made of glass at all
exercise: What might happen if you put a hot ordinary glass dish into cold water? | It might shatter | It would turn soft | It would get stronger | It would turn cloudy and melt
exercise: What can lightning make when it strikes sand? | Glass tubes called fulgurites | Small gold nuggets | Little diamonds | Puffs of coloured sand blown high into the air
exercise: What is obsidian? | Natural glass from a volcano | A kind of hard, shiny black plastic | Black pottery found in old caves | A black metal dug from deep mines
exercise: Why is sea glass smooth and frosty? | Waves and sand rub it for many years | The salt melts its sharp edges at once | Fish lick it smooth | It was made smooth in a factory
exercise: How should you clear up a broken glass? | Sweep it carefully with a brush and dustpan | Pick all the pieces up quickly with bare hands | Kick the pieces under the sofa | Vacuum it while barefoot
exercise: What happens when a car side window is smashed? | It crumbles into small blunt pieces | It breaks into long, very sharp spikes | It bends like rubber | It melts into a puddle
exercise: How is a clay mug made hard? | It is baked in a very hot kiln | It is left to dry in the fridge | It is painted with a hard paint | It is dipped in a bucket of cold water
exercise: What does a glaze do for a mug? | Gives it a thin glass coat that keeps water in | Makes the mug much lighter and easier to lift | Keeps the tea hot for hours | Stops it breaking when dropped
exercise: Why is glass so good for recycling? | It can be melted and remade again and again | It turns into sand by itself in a year | It only needs to be washed and dried | It turns into plastic when melted
exercise: How is a phone screen made tougher than window glass? | The glass is treated to harden its surface | It is made of a thin, see-through slice of diamond | It is covered with ice | It is made of metal painted clear
exercise: How do optical fibres carry light around bends? | The light keeps bouncing inside the glass | The light is pulled round the bends by magnets | The glass glows from the inside | The light is pumped like water
exercise: What carries most of the internet under the oceans? | Glass fibres in cables | Radio waves through the water | Copper water pipes on the seabed | Satellites under the sea
test: Max: "old church windows are thicker at the bottom 'cos glass slowly drips down". What's true? | They were made uneven; glass does not flow | He's right, glass is a very slow liquid that drips | The bottom got thicker from dirt | The top was worn down by the rain
test: "smashed a glass, crap, I'll just grab the bits quick". What should you do instead? | Use a brush and dustpan, not bare hands | Grab the big bits with wet hands | Stand on them to make them smaller | Throw a towel over them and walk off
test: If the whole windscreen of a car broke into tiny pieces in a crash, what would go wrong? | Pieces could fly at people inside | The car would go faster | Nothing, that is how it is meant to work | The car would become waterproof
test: Gran's mug has no glaze inside. What will happen when she fills it with tea? | Some tea will soak into the clay | The tea will freeze at once | The mug will melt in the heat | The tea will turn into glaze
test: A wizard in a cartoon turns sand into glass just by waving a wand. What does it really take? | Melting sand with other things at great heat | Mixing sand with cold water | Squeezing sand very, very hard by hand | Leaving sand out in the hot sun for a whole day
test: Ruby finds a frosty green pebble on the beach: "is it a gem?" Most likely? | Old bottle glass rubbed by the sea for years | A real emerald from a sunken pirate shipwreck | A piece of green plastic from a toy | A frozen drop of sea water
test: "my phone's 'unbreakable glass', so I can drop it on its corner, no problem". Right? | No, a hit on the corner can still crack it | Yes, toughened glass never breaks | Yes, corners are the strongest part | No, it only cracks when it is wet
test: If people stopped recycling bottles, what would be needed more? | Sand and energy to make new glass | Much more oil for making new plastic bottles | More clay for new mugs | Less of anything at all
test: Which is natural glass? | Obsidian from a volcano | A pottery plate | A phone screen | A clay brick
test: Your video call to Australia travels under the ocean. In what? | Light in thin glass fibres | Sound travelling in metal pipes | Water pumped in rubber hoses | Electricity in the sea water
test: Why can you not just put a clay pot from the art class into the kiln wet and soggy? | It must dry first or it may crack | Wet clay turns into glass in the heat | The kiln would turn it to mud | Wet clay never gets hard
exam: Identify the principal raw materials from which ordinary window glass is manufactured. | Sand, soda and limestone | Clay, water and straw | Iron ore and carbon | Oil and natural gas
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the arrangement of particles in glass? | The particles are disordered rather than arranged in crystals | The particles are arranged in perfect cubes | The particles flow slowly downwards over centuries | The particles are separated by pockets of air
exam: It is given that a heated vessel of ordinary glass is plunged into cold water. Select the outcome most consistent with the established facts. | The vessel may fracture owing to uneven contraction | The vessel will soften and deform into a new shape | The vessel will become stronger | The vessel will dissolve in the water
exam: Ascertain the function of a glaze upon an article of pottery. | It forms a thin vitreous coating that renders the article waterproof | It reduces the mass of the article considerably | It conducts heat away from the contents | It renders the article unbreakable
